(function(){(()=>{"use strict";const e=(r,a,i,n,s,o,t)=>({g:r,p:a,n:i,f:n,how:s,ex:o,...t?{fig:t}:{}});window.PhysicaRankSkillsJee={chapter:"Experimental Skills",secs:["Vernier callipers and least count","Screw gauge and metre scale","Errors and significant figures","Simple pendulum and graphs","Young\u2019s modulus, surface tension and viscosity","Speed of sound and specific heat","Mirror, lens, slab and prism","Metre bridge and potentiometer","Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter","Diodes, multimeter and graphs"],table:[["Metre scale","least count 1 mm (0.1 cm)"],["Vernier callipers (10 VSD = 9 MSD)","0.1 mm = 0.01 cm"],["Screw gauge (pitch 0.5 mm, 50 divisions)","0.01 mm = 0.001 cm"],["Spherometer (pitch 1 mm, 100 divisions)","0.01 mm"],["Stopwatch (digital)","0.01 s; hand-held: about 0.1 s"],["Travelling microscope","about 0.01 mm to 0.001 cm"],["Common balance and weight box","smallest weight 1 mg to 10 mg"],["Laboratory thermometer","1 \xB0C"],["Ammeter or voltmeter (analogue)","smallest division of the scale"],["Metre bridge wire","1 mm on a 100 cm wire"],["Measuring cylinder","1 mL or 0.5 mL (volume)"],["Digital multimeter","last digit shown (for example 0.01 V)"]],formulas:[e("Vernier callipers and least count",1,"Least count of a vernier","LC = 1 MSD \u2212 1 VSD = {1 MSD;N} = {value of one main scale division;number of vernier divisions} \xB7 for N VSD = (N \u2212 1) MSD, LC = {1 MSD;N} \xB7 with 1 MSD = 1 mm and N = 10, LC = 0.1 mm = 0.01 cm \xB7 if N VSD = (N + 1) MSD (reverse vernier), LC = 1 VSD \u2212 1 MSD, again {1 MSD;N} \xB7 the least count is the smallest length the instrument can read","Write what 1 MSD is, then the number of vernier divisions N. The least count is 1 MSD divided by N. Do not use the length of the vernier scale.","1 MSD = 0.5 mm and N = 50 gives LC = 0.5 \xF7 50 = 0.01 mm. A vernier with 1 MSD = 1 mm and 20 divisions has LC = 0.05 mm.","xsvern"),e("Vernier callipers and least count",1,"Reading a vernier","reading = MSR + (VSR \xD7 LC) \xB7 MSR = the main scale reading just before the vernier zero \xB7 VSR = the number of the vernier line that coincides with a main scale line \xB7 reading in cm: MSR (cm) + VSR \xD7 0.01 cm \xB7 the final answer is written with the same number of decimal places as the least count","Read the main scale to the left of the vernier zero. Then find the one vernier line that is in line with a main scale line. Multiply that line number by LC and add.","Zero of the vernier just after 23 mm and the 6th line coinciding: reading = 23 + 6 \xD7 0.1 = 23.6 mm = 2.36 cm.","xsvern"),e("Vernier callipers and least count",1,"Zero error and zero correction","zero error e: the reading when the jaws are closed \xB7 zero correction = \u2212e \xB7 true reading = observed reading \u2212 e \xB7 positive error (vernier zero to the right of the main zero, nth line coincides): e = + n \xD7 LC \xB7 negative error (vernier zero to the left, nth line coincides): e = \u2212(N \u2212 n) \xD7 LC \xB7 the same rules hold for a screw gauge, with the circular scale in place of the vernier","Close the jaws first and note e with its sign. Always subtract e from every observed reading: a positive e is subtracted, a negative e is added.","Closed jaws: 4th line coincides with the vernier to the left of zero, N = 10: e = \u2212(10 \u2212 4) \xD7 0.1 = \u22120.6 mm. An observed 23.6 mm gives 23.6 \u2212 (\u22120.6) = 24.2 mm. For a positive e = +0.2 mm the true value is 23.6 \u2212 0.2 = 23.4 mm."),e("Vernier callipers and least count",2,"What the vernier measures and how","outside jaws: external diameter or length of a block \xB7 inside jaws: internal diameter of a tube or a hollow \xB7 the strip at the end: depth of a cavity or a beaker \xB7 volume of a cylinder V = \u03C0r\xB2l = {\u03C0d\xB2l;4} \xB7 hold the object gently between the jaws; do not press, and take readings at several places","For a cylinder, measure d at two perpendicular directions and l at three places, then average. The percentage error in V adds 2 \xD7 (\u0394d/d) and \u0394l/l.","d = 2.36 cm and l = 5.40 cm give V = \u03C0 \xD7 2.36\xB2 \xD7 5.40 \xF7 4 = 23.6 cm\xB3 (three significant figures)."),e("Screw gauge and metre scale",1,"Pitch and least count of a screw gauge","pitch p = {distance moved on the main scale;number of full turns} \xB7 LC = {pitch;number of divisions on the circular scale} \xB7 pitch 0.5 mm and 50 divisions: LC = 0.01 mm = 0.001 cm \xB7 pitch 1 mm and 100 divisions: LC = 0.01 mm \xB7 one full turn of the thimble moves the screw by one pitch","Find the pitch from the data (distance moved in a few turns divided by the number of turns). Then divide by the circular scale divisions.","The screw advances 2.5 mm in 5 turns: pitch = 0.5 mm. With 50 divisions, LC = 0.5 \xF7 50 = 0.01 mm.","xsscrew"),e("Screw gauge and metre scale",1,"Reading of a screw gauge","reading = MSR + (CSR \xD7 LC) \xB7 MSR = the last main scale (linear scale) mark uncovered by the thimble, including a half-millimetre mark if it is visible \xB7 CSR = the circular scale division that is on the reference line \xB7 corrected reading = reading \u2212 zero error \xB7 the screw gauge is read in mm and changed to cm or m at the end","Count the whole mm and the half-mm mark visible on the sleeve, then add CSR \xD7 LC. Take care when the thimble edge is almost on a mark: use the circular reading to decide.","Pitch 0.5 mm, 50 divisions, MSR = 4.5 mm and CSR = 28: reading = 4.5 + 28 \xD7 0.01 = 4.78 mm. With zero error +0.03 mm the true diameter is 4.75 mm.","xsscrew"),e("Screw gauge and metre scale",1,"Zero error of a screw gauge","close the gauge and read the circular scale against the reference line \xB7 reading n between 0 and about 25: positive error e = +n \xD7 LC \xB7 reading n near the end of the scale (for example 97 on 100 divisions): negative error e = \u2212(N \u2212 n) \xD7 LC, N = number of circular divisions \xB7 true diameter = observed reading \u2212 e \xB7 so a negative error is added to the observed reading","Read the closed gauge first and write e with its sign. Small reading: positive. Reading close to N: negative, equal to (n \u2212 N) \xD7 LC.","Closed gauge reads 97 on a 100-division scale (LC 0.01 mm): e = (97 \u2212 100) \xD7 0.01 = \u22120.03 mm. An observed 4.78 mm becomes 4.78 + 0.03 = 4.81 mm. A closed reading of 3 divisions gives e = +0.03 mm."),e("Screw gauge and metre scale",2,"Backlash and the ratchet","backlash error: the screw turns a little without moving when the direction of turning is reversed \xB7 always turn the screw in the same direction while taking readings \xB7 use the ratchet (friction head) so that the pressure on the wire is the same each time \xB7 do not press the wire: a soft wire gets flattened and the reading is too small","A screw gauge reading is a measurement of one diameter at one place. Rotate the wire and take readings in two perpendicular directions, at three places, then find the mean.","Diameters of 0.42, 0.41, 0.43, 0.42 mm give a mean of 0.42 mm. Using the mean in the area A = \u03C0d\xB2/4 keeps the random error small."),e("Screw gauge and metre scale",1,"Metre scale, spherometer and parallax","metre scale: LC = 1 mm, so a length of 50.0 cm has an uncertainty of 0.1 cm at each end (read both ends) \xB7 parallax: keep the eye directly above the mark \xB7 a worn end of the scale gives an error: start from the 1.00 cm mark and subtract \xB7 spherometer: R = {l\xB2;6h} + {h;2}, with l the distance between two legs and h the height of the central screw","Read both ends of a length and subtract so that the zero error of the scale cancels. For the spherometer, always take h from the difference of the readings on a flat plate and on the curved surface.","Legs 3.0 cm apart (l = 3.0 cm) and h = 0.1 cm: R = {l\xB2;6h} + {h;2} = 9 \xF7 0.6 + 0.05 = 15.05 cm."),e("Errors and significant figures",1,"Absolute, relative and percentage error","mean a = {a\u2081 + a\u2082 + \u2026 + a\u2099;n} \xB7 absolute error in one reading \u0394a\u1D62 = |a \u2212 a\u1D62| \xB7 mean absolute error \u0394a = {\u03A3|\u0394a\u1D62|;n} \xB7 relative (fractional) error = {\u0394a;a} \xB7 percentage error = {\u0394a;a} \xD7 100 % \xB7 the result is written as a \xB1 \u0394a \xB7 the least count error of an instrument is its LC","Find the mean first, then the deviation of each reading from the mean, then the mean of the deviations. Report the error with 1 or 2 significant figures.","Readings 2.63, 2.56, 2.42, 2.71, 2.80: mean = 2.62; deviations 0.01, 0.06, 0.20, 0.09, 0.18; mean deviation = 0.11. The result is 2.62 \xB1 0.11, a percentage error of 4.1 %."),e("Errors and significant figures",1,"Error in a sum or a difference","Z = A + B or Z = A \u2212 B: \u0394Z = \u0394A + \u0394B \xB7 the absolute errors add in both cases \xB7 a difference of two nearly equal numbers has a large relative error because Z becomes small \xB7 the maximum error is the sum, never the difference","Add the absolute errors (not the percentages) for a sum or a difference. Convert to a percentage only at the end.","Temperatures 20.0 \xB1 0.5 \xB0C and 50.0 \xB1 0.5 \xB0C: the rise is 30.0 \xB1 1.0 \xB0C, a relative error of 3.3 %."),e("Errors and significant figures",1,"Error in a product or a quotient","Z = A \xD7 B or Z = {A;B}: {\u0394Z;Z} = {\u0394A;A} + {\u0394B;B} \xB7 relative errors add (for both multiplication and division) \xB7 for a constant factor (like 4\u03C0) no extra error comes in \xB7 for several quantities, add all the relative errors","Write the relative errors as fractions or percentages and add them. Do not subtract for a quotient.","R = V/I with V = 5.0 \xB1 0.1 V and I = 2.0 \xB1 0.05 A: {\u0394R;R} = 0.02 + 0.025 = 0.045, so R = 2.5 \u03A9 with \u0394R = 0.11 \u03A9 (4.5 %)."),e("Errors and significant figures",1,"Error in a power","Z = A\u207F: {\u0394Z;Z} = |n| {\u0394A;A} \xB7 Z = {A\u1D56 B\u1D60;C\u02B3}: {\u0394Z;Z} = p{\u0394A;A} + q{\u0394B;B} + r{\u0394C;C} \xB7 the sign of the power does not matter: the factor is the size of the power \xB7 a square root has a factor {1;2}","Multiply each relative error by the size of its power and add everything. A quantity with a high power has the largest effect on the error.","Z = x\xB2y\xB3/z with errors 1 %, 2 %, 3 % in x, y, z: error in Z = 2 \xD7 1 + 3 \xD7 2 + 1 \xD7 3 = 11 %."),e("Errors and significant figures",1,"Error in g, density and similar results","g from the pendulum: {\u0394g;g} = {\u0394L;L} + 2{\u0394T;T} \xB7 density of a cylinder: {\u0394\u03C1;\u03C1} = {\u0394m;m} + 2{\u0394d;d} + {\u0394l;l} \xB7 resistivity \u03C1 = {\u03C0d\xB2R;4L}: {\u0394\u03C1;\u03C1} = 2{\u0394d;d} + {\u0394R;R} + {\u0394L;L} \xB7 the quantity with the highest power or the largest relative error dominates","Write the formula, list the power of each measured quantity, and add the relative errors with those powers. Then decide which measurement should be improved first.","L = 100.0 \xB1 0.1 cm and T = 2.00 \xB1 0.005 s (20 oscillations in 40.0 \xB1 0.1 s): {\u0394g;g} = 0.001 + 2 \xD7 0.0025 = 0.006, that is 0.6 %."),e("Errors and significant figures",1,"Least count error and the measurement of time","least count error = LC of the instrument \xB7 for n oscillations timed together, the error in T is {LC of the watch;n} \xB7 timing 20 oscillations reduces the error in T by a factor of 20 \xB7 more readings reduce the random error but not the systematic error","To measure a small quantity, repeat or multiply it (time many oscillations, count many turns of a wire, use a thick stack of sheets) so that the least count error becomes a small fraction.","A stopwatch with LC 0.1 s: for one oscillation of 2.0 s the error is 5 %; for 20 oscillations (40.0 s) the error in T is 0.1 \xF7 20 = 0.005 s, 0.25 %."),e("Errors and significant figures",2,"Systematic and random errors","systematic errors have one sign and one cause: instrument (zero error, wrong calibration), personal (parallax) or imperfection of method; they can be removed by correction \xB7 random errors change in sign and size from reading to reading; they are reduced by taking many readings and averaging \xB7 gross errors are mistakes (a wrong reading); they are discarded","Name the type of error from its behaviour: always the same direction means systematic; scattered both ways means random.","A screw gauge with zero error +0.03 mm: every reading is high by 0.03 mm (systematic, removed by correction). Differences of \xB10.01 mm between readings are random."),e("Errors and significant figures",1,"Significant figures and rounding","all non-zero digits are significant \xB7 zeros between digits are significant \xB7 leading zeros are not (0.0042 has 2) \xB7 trailing zeros after the decimal point are significant (4.20 has 3) \xB7 sum or difference: keep as many decimal places as the number with the fewest \xB7 product or quotient: keep as many significant figures as the number with the fewest \xB7 exact numbers (2, \u03C0, counts) do not limit the result","Do the arithmetic in full and round only at the end. In an addition count decimal places; in a multiplication count significant figures. Round up when the next digit is 5 or more.","12.5 + 3.27 = 15.77, written 15.8 (one decimal place). 2.5 \xD7 3.41 = 8.525, written 8.5 (two significant figures)."),e("Simple pendulum and graphs",1,"Time period and the value of g","T = 2\u03C0\u221A({L;g}) \xB7 g = {4\u03C0\xB2L;T\xB2} \xB7 L = effective length = length of the thread l + radius r of the bob (to the centre of mass) \xB7 T is found from n oscillations: T = {t;n} (n = 20 to 30), counted from the mean position \xB7 T does not depend on the mass of the bob","Measure the length from the point of suspension to the centre of the bob. Put the values in g = 4\u03C0\xB2L/T\xB2. Time more than 20 oscillations.","L = 1.00 m and 20 oscillations take 40.2 s: T = 2.01 s, g = 4 \xD7 9.87 \xD7 1.00 \xF7 2.01\xB2 = 9.77 m/s\xB2.","xspend"),e("Simple pendulum and graphs",1,"Effect of the bob, the thread and the amplitude","use a small, heavy, spherical bob and a thin, light, inextensible thread \xB7 the bob radius must be added to the thread length \xB7 the amplitude should be small (\u03B8 below about 10\xB0 to 15\xB0), since T \u2248 T\u2080(1 + {\u03B8\xB2;16}) rises for large \u03B8 \xB7 the thread should be fixed between two split cork pieces so that the point of suspension is fixed \xB7 if r is ignored, g comes out too small \xB7 a thread that stretches gives an error in L","Check each condition of the formula: small angle, point mass, fixed support, no air resistance. A violation means a systematic error.","For \u03B8 = 0.2 rad (about 11.5\xB0): T is larger than T\u2080 by {\u03B8\xB2;16} = 0.04 \xF7 16 = 0.25 %, which makes g lower by about 0.5 %."),e("Simple pendulum and graphs",1,"The T\xB2\u2013L graph","T\xB2 = {4\u03C0\xB2;g}L \xB7 the graph of T\xB2 against L is a straight line through the origin \xB7 slope = {T\xB2;L} = {4\u03C0\xB2;g}, so g = {4\u03C0\xB2;slope} \xB7 if T\xB2 is plotted against the thread length l, the line cuts the l axis at \u2212r (negative) because L = l + r \xB7 the graph gives a better g than a single reading because the random errors average out \xB7 the graph of T against \u221AL is also a straight line","Take two far-apart points on the best-fit line (not data points) and find the slope. If the line does not pass through the origin, the intercept shows a constant error in L (such as the bob radius).","Slope = 4.0 s\xB2/m gives g = 4 \xD7 9.87 \xF7 4.0 = 9.87 m/s\xB2. The length of a seconds pendulum (T = 2 s) is L = g/\u03C0\xB2 = 0.993 m.","xspend"),e("Simple pendulum and graphs",2,"Seconds pendulum and effect of changes","a seconds pendulum has T = 2 s and L = 0.993 m (about 99.3 cm) at g = 9.8 m/s\xB2 \xB7 T \u221D \u221AL, T \u221D 1/\u221Ag \xB7 if L is made four times, T doubles \xB7 a bob filled with a liquid that drains out first raises and then lowers T (the centre of mass moves) \xB7 the pendulum in a lift accelerating up: g\u2032 = g + a","Use ratios for a change of L or g: T\u2032/T = \u221A(L\u2032/L) or \u221A(g/g\u2032).","L is changed from 25 cm to 100 cm: T doubles. In a lift with a = 2.0 m/s\xB2 upward, T becomes T \xD7 \u221A(9.8 \xF7 11.8) = 0.91 T."),e("Young\u2019s modulus, surface tension and viscosity",1,"Young\u2019s modulus by Searle\u2019s method","Y = {F/A;\u0394L/L} = {MgL;\u03C0r\xB2l} = {4MgL;\u03C0D\xB2l} \xB7 M = load added, L = original length of the experimental wire, r or D = radius or diameter (screw gauge), l = extension (spirit level and micrometer screw) \xB7 a second, identical wire (reference wire) carries a constant load and keeps the spirit level horizontal against temperature and support changes \xB7 plot load against extension: the slope M/l gives Y = {gL;\u03C0r\xB2} \xD7 slope \xB7 take readings for loading and unloading and find the mean to remove elastic fatigue","Find the slope of the load\u2013extension graph (M against l), then put it in Y = (gL/\u03C0r\xB2) \xD7 (M/l). The diameter appears squared, so it must be measured carefully.","L = 2.0 m, r = 0.4 mm, a 1 kg load gives l = 0.20 mm: Y = 9.8 \xD7 1 \xD7 2.0 \xF7 (\u03C0 \xD7 (0.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 \xD7 0.20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 1.95 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2 (the value for steel)."),e("Young\u2019s modulus, surface tension and viscosity",1,"Surface tension by capillary rise","T = {rh\u03C1g;2 cos\u03B8} \u2248 {rh\u03C1g;2} for a wetting liquid (\u03B8 \u2248 0\xB0) \xB7 r = radius of the tube (travelling microscope or screw gauge), h = rise measured from the free surface \xB7 more exactly h is corrected for the liquid above the lowest point of the meniscus: h + {r;3} \xB7 the tube must be clean and vertical; a narrower tube gives a larger h \xB7 for mercury, \u03B8 \u2248 135\xB0 and the level is depressed","Measure h from the level of the liquid outside the tube to the bottom of the meniscus, add r/3 for accuracy, and use the formula. The tube radius must be measured at the level of the meniscus.","Water in a tube of r = 0.50 mm rises to h = 3.0 cm: T = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.030 \xD7 1000 \xD7 9.8 \xF7 2 = 0.0735 N/m = 7.35 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 N/m."),e("Young\u2019s modulus, surface tension and viscosity",1,"Viscosity by Stokes\u2019 method","viscous force F = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv \xB7 terminal velocity v = {2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g;9\u03B7} \xB7 \u03B7 = {2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g;9v} \xB7 \u03C1 = density of the ball, \u03C3 = density of the liquid \xB7 v is the speed measured between two marks after the ball has reached terminal speed (time over a fixed distance) \xB7 the radius of the ball is found with a screw gauge \xB7 the tube must be wide and the ball small; the viscosity falls as temperature rises","Check terminal speed first (equal time over equal distances). Then use \u03B7 = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/9v with the radius in metres and the densities in kg/m\xB3.","Steel ball (\u03C1 = 7800 kg/m\xB3, r = 1.0 mm) in glycerine (\u03C3 = 1260 kg/m\xB3) with v = 0.016 m/s: \u03B7 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 6540 \xD7 9.8 \xF7 (9 \xD7 0.016) = 0.89 Pa s."),e("Young\u2019s modulus, surface tension and viscosity",2,"Stokes\u2019 law checks and the effect of size","when v is terminal: weight = buoyancy + viscous force, {4;3}\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1g = {4;3}\u03C0r\xB3\u03C3g + 6\u03C0\u03B7rv \xB7 v \u221D r\xB2 (a double radius gives four times v) \xB7 the unit of \u03B7 is Pa s = kg m\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9 \xB7 the ratio of terminal speeds of two balls of the same material in the same liquid is {v\u2081;v\u2082} = {r\u2081\xB2;r\u2082\xB2}","If a question gives two radii in one liquid, write the ratio of the speeds as the square of the ratio of the radii.","A ball of twice the radius in the same liquid reaches a terminal speed 4 times as large."),e("Speed of sound and specific heat",1,"Resonance tube: first and second resonance","closed pipe: the first resonance at L\u2081 + e = {\u03BB;4} and the second at L\u2082 + e = {3\u03BB;4} \xB7 \u03BB = 2(L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081) \xB7 v = f\u03BB = 2f(L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081) \xB7 end correction e = {L\u2082 \u2212 3L\u2081;2} \u2248 0.6 r (r = radius of the tube) \xB7 the end correction is removed when the difference of the two lengths is used \xB7 the fork must be vibrating freely; the water level is adjusted until the loudest sound is heard","Use two resonances, so that e does not enter. Find \u03BB from L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081 and v from f \xD7 \u03BB. The temperature of the air matters: v increases about 0.6 m/s per \xB0C.","f = 512 Hz, L\u2081 = 16.0 cm and L\u2082 = 50.0 cm: \u03BB = 68 cm, v = 512 \xD7 0.68 = 348 m/s, and e = (50.0 \u2212 48.0) \xF7 2 = 1.0 cm.","xsres"),e("Speed of sound and specific heat",2,"Corrections and sources of error in the resonance tube","v at 0 \xB0C: v\u2080 = v \u2212 0.61t (t in \xB0C) with v = 331 + 0.61t \xB7 v\u2080 \u2248 331 m/s at 0 \xB0C \xB7 v \u221D \u221AT (absolute temperature) \xB7 neglecting e gives a wrong v if only L\u2081 is used: \u03BB = 4L\u2081 is too small by 4e \xB7 humidity increases v \xB7 the position of the resonance is found by the loudest note, not by the first sound","Always use the two-length formula in exact work. If an answer uses only L\u2081, correct it with L\u2081 + e = \u03BB/4.","At 27 \xB0C, v = 331 + 0.61 \xD7 27 = 347 m/s. The measured 348 m/s agrees within the error."),e("Speed of sound and specific heat",1,"Specific heat by the method of mixtures","heat lost by the hot solid = heat gained by the water and the calorimeter \xB7 m_s c_s(T\u2081 \u2212 T) = (m_w c_w + m_c c_c)(T \u2212 T\u2080) \xB7 T\u2081 = temperature of the hot solid, T\u2080 = initial temperature of the water, T = the highest steady temperature of the mixture \xB7 water equivalent of the calorimeter w = m_c c_c \xB7 c_s = {(m_w + w)(T \u2212 T\u2080);m_s(T\u2081 \u2212 T)}","Write the energy balance in one line, include the calorimeter, and solve for c_s. Transfer the solid quickly and stir.","100 g solid at 100 \xB0C in a calorimeter (50 g, c = 0.09 cal/g \xB0C) with 150 g water at 25.0 \xB0C; the final temperature is 30.0 \xB0C: c_s = (150 + 4.5) \xD7 5.0 \xF7 (100 \xD7 70) = 0.110 cal/g \xB0C."),e("Speed of sound and specific heat",2,"Specific heat of a liquid and the losses","liquid: m_s c_s(T\u2081 \u2212 T) = (m_l c_l + w)(T \u2212 T\u2080) with a solid of known c_s \xB7 c_l = {m_s c_s(T\u2081 \u2212 T) \u2212 w(T \u2212 T\u2080);m_l(T \u2212 T\u2080)} \xB7 heat loss by radiation is reduced by a polished calorimeter placed in a cotton-lined outer jacket, a lid, and a final temperature close to the room temperature","Give the liquid the position of the water in the balance equation. Sources of error: heat lost during the transfer, an unstirred liquid and a thermometer that is too slow.","Copper block (c = 0.093 cal/g \xB0C, 200 g) at 100 \xB0C into 150 g of liquid at 20 \xB0C with w = 10 g, final temperature 30 \xB0C: c_l = (200 \xD7 0.093 \xD7 70 \xF7 10 \u2212 10) \xF7 150 = 0.80 cal/g \xB0C."),e("Mirror, lens, slab and prism",1,"Focal length of a concave mirror: u\u2013v method","1/v + 1/u = 1/f with the Cartesian sign convention (object distance u negative) \xB7 real image on the same side: v negative \xB7 f = {uv;u + v} \xB7 the average of several (u, v) pairs gives f \xB7 the 1/u\u20131/v graph is a straight line of slope \u22121 meeting both axes at 1/f (magnitude) \xB7 the u\u2013v graph is a hyperbola; the line v = u meets it at u = v = 2f = R (the centre of curvature)","Use the Cartesian sign convention throughout and find f from many readings. Place the object at the centre of curvature to get an image of the same size, then R = u.","u = \u221230 cm and v = \u221260 cm: f = (\u221230)(\u221260) \xF7 (\u221290) = \u221220 cm (concave, f = 20 cm in magnitude). For u = v = \u221240 cm, f = \u221220 cm."),e("Mirror, lens, slab and prism",1,"Focal length of a convex lens: u\u2013v method","1/v \u2212 1/u = 1/f \xB7 u negative, v positive for a real image \xB7 f = {uv;u \u2212 v} \xB7 plot 1/v against 1/u: a straight line with intercepts 1/f on both axes (v intercept = +1/f, u intercept = \u22121/f) \xB7 plot v against u: a hyperbola with u = v = \xB12f at the point where the image is as large as the object \xB7 the image is real and inverted for u greater than f","Use the sign convention and many readings. Avoid the readings very close to f. For a graph, find the intercept on the 1/v axis: it gives 1/f at once.","u = \u221230 cm and v = +60 cm: 1/f = 1/60 + 1/30 = 3/60, so f = 20 cm. A 1/v\u20131/u graph with the 1/v intercept 0.050 cm\u207B\xB9 gives f = 20 cm."),e("Mirror, lens, slab and prism",2,"Displacement method (conjugate foci)","object and screen fixed at distance D > 4f \xB7 the lens has two positions, a distance d apart, which give a sharp image \xB7 f = {D\xB2 \u2212 d\xB2;4D} \xB7 magnification at the two positions: m\u2081 m\u2082 = 1 \xB7 the size of the object is \u221A(I\u2081I\u2082) \xB7 for D = 4f only one position exists (d = 0)","Measure D (object to screen) and d (the shift of the lens). The formula removes the need to find the exact centre of the lens, which makes this method more accurate.","D = 100 cm and d = 20 cm: f = (10000 \u2212 400) \xF7 400 = 24 cm."),e("Mirror, lens, slab and prism",1,"Refractive index of a glass slab","n = {sin i;sin r} \xB7 the sin i\u2013sin r graph is a straight line through the origin with slope n \xB7 lateral shift = {t sin(i \u2212 r);cos r} (t = thickness) \xB7 with a travelling microscope: n = {real thickness;apparent thickness} (the apparent depth is smaller) \xB7 the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray","Draw the normal at the pins with a fine pencil. Plot sin i against sin r and take the slope; for the microscope method note three readings (mark on the paper, on the slab, on the powder).","i = 45\xB0 and r = 28.1\xB0: n = 0.7071 \xF7 0.4714 = 1.50. A slab of real thickness 3.0 cm with an apparent thickness of 2.0 cm: n = 1.5."),e("Mirror, lens, slab and prism",1,"Prism: the i\u2013\u03B4 curve and the refractive index","\u03B4 = i + e \u2212 A \xB7 the i\u2013\u03B4 graph is U-shaped with the lowest point at the minimum deviation \u03B4m, where i = e = {A + \u03B4m;2} and r = {A;2} \xB7 n = {sin((A + \u03B4m)/2);sin(A/2)} \xB7 for a thin prism, \u03B4 = (n \u2212 1)A \xB7 every deviation larger than \u03B4m is obtained at two values of i (one is i, the other is e)","Draw the graph and read \u03B4m at the lowest point. Then use the formula for n. Pins must be 6 to 10 cm apart for the lines to be accurate.","A = 60\xB0 and \u03B4m = 37\xB0: n = sin 48.5\xB0 \xF7 sin 30\xB0 = 0.749 \xF7 0.5 = 1.50.","xsid"),e("Metre bridge and potentiometer",1,"Metre bridge: resistance of a wire","Wheatstone balance: {R;S} = {l;100 \u2212 l} \xB7 R = {S l;100 \u2212 l} \xB7 the unknown R is in the left gap, the known resistance box S in the right gap \xB7 the balance point should be near the middle (30 to 70 cm) for the best sensitivity \xB7 swap R and S and average to remove the end errors; do not press the jockey hard or heat the wire by a long current","Write R/S = l/(100 \u2212 l), put l in cm and solve for R. Adjust S so that l lies between 40 cm and 60 cm.","S = 3.0 \u03A9 and l = 40.0 cm: R = 3.0 \xD7 40 \xF7 60 = 2.0 \u03A9.","xsmb"),e("Metre bridge and potentiometer",1,"Resistivity of the wire by the metre bridge","R = \u03C1{L;A} = {4\u03C1L;\u03C0d\xB2} so \u03C1 = {\u03C0d\xB2R;4L} \xB7 d is measured with a screw gauge, L with a metre scale, R with the bridge \xB7 the percentage error in \u03C1 is 2{\u0394d;d} + {\u0394R;R} + {\u0394L;L} (d has the largest weight) \xB7 the specific resistance rises with temperature","Measure d at several places with the screw gauge and use the mean. Then \u03C1 = \u03C0d\xB2R/4L with all lengths in metres.","R = 2.0 \u03A9, d = 0.40 mm, L = 1.0 m: \u03C1 = 2.0 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 (0.40 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 \xF7 (4 \xD7 1.0) = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9 m."),e("Metre bridge and potentiometer",1,"Potentiometer: comparison of emfs","potential gradient k = {V;L} = {I \u03C1;A} (volt per metre) along the wire \xB7 at the null point E = k l \xB7 {E\u2081;E\u2082} = {l\u2081;l\u2082} \xB7 the driver cell has emf greater than both E\u2081 and E\u2082 and the positive poles of all cells are joined to the same end of the wire \xB7 no current flows from the test cell at balance, so the potentiometer measures the emf itself, not the terminal voltage","Use the same wire and the same current: the ratio of the balance lengths equals the ratio of the emfs. The two-way key swaps E\u2081 and E\u2082 without changing anything else.","l\u2081 = 75.0 cm for one cell and l\u2082 = 50.0 cm for the other: E\u2081 \xF7 E\u2082 = 1.5. If the first emf is 1.5 V, the second is 1.0 V.","xspot"),e("Metre bridge and potentiometer",1,"Potentiometer: internal resistance of a cell","balance length l\u2081 with the cell on open circuit (emf E = k l\u2081) \xB7 with a resistance R across the cell, balance length l\u2082 (terminal voltage V = k l\u2082) \xB7 r = R({l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082;l\u2082}) = R({E;V} \u2212 1) \xB7 the internal resistance is found without drawing a current from the cell at balance \xB7 choose R so that l\u2082 is not very small","Write E = k l\u2081 and V = k l\u2082, and divide to get E/V = l\u2081/l\u2082. Then r = (E/V \u2212 1)R.","l\u2081 = 60 cm, l\u2082 = 50 cm, R = 10 \u03A9: r = 10 \xD7 10 \xF7 50 = 2.0 \u03A9.","xspot"),e("Metre bridge and potentiometer",2,"Sensitivity of the potentiometer","sensitivity rises with a longer wire (a smaller potential gradient k) \xB7 k = {V;L} falls when the series resistance in the driver circuit is raised \xB7 a potentiometer is better than a voltmeter for emf because it draws no current at balance (infinite resistance) \xB7 the null (balance) point is found without a deflection, which needs a sensitive galvanometer with a protective resistance in series at first","Use a long wire and a low k for small emfs; remove the protective resistance when the balance point is close.","A 10 m wire with 2.0 V across it has k = 0.2 V/m. A 1 mm shift in the balance length therefore means 0.2 mV."),e("Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",1,"Galvanometer resistance by half deflection","close K\u2081 only and adjust R to bring the deflection to \u03B8 (full scale): I_g = {E;R + G} \xB7 then close K\u2082 and adjust the shunt S until the deflection is {\u03B8;2} \xB7 G = {R S;R \u2212 S} \xB7 if R \u226B G the shunt is nearly equal to G (G \u2248 S) \xB7 the cell and resistance R are chosen so that R is a few thousand ohm","Use the two steps: R sets the full deflection; S halves it. Put R and S in G = RS/(R \u2212 S). Do not use a very small R, because G would not be negligible.","R = 3000 \u03A9 and S = 100 \u03A9: G = 3000 \xD7 100 \xF7 2900 = 103 \u03A9 (about S)."),e("Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",1,"Figure of merit of a galvanometer","figure of merit k = {I;\u03B8} = {E;(R + G)\u03B8} (ampere per division) \xB7 current sensitivity = {\u03B8;I} = {1;k} \xB7 the full-scale current I_g = kN for N divisions \xB7 k is the current needed for one division of deflection","Find \u03B8 at the first step (without the shunt), then k = E/[(R + G)\u03B8] with R + G in ohm and E in volt.","E = 2 V, R = 4000 \u03A9, G = 100 \u03A9, \u03B8 = 20 divisions: k = 2 \xF7 (4100 \xD7 20) = 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 A per division."),e("Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",1,"Conversion to an ammeter","shunt S in parallel with the galvanometer \xB7 I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g)S \xB7 S = {I_g G;I \u2212 I_g} \xB7 the resistance of the ammeter is {GS;G + S} (very small) \xB7 the ammeter is joined in series \xB7 a smaller shunt gives a larger range","The shunt takes the extra current I \u2212 I_g. Write I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g)S and solve for S.","G = 100 \u03A9, I_g = 1 mA and range 1 A: S = 0.001 \xD7 100 \xF7 0.999 = 0.100 \u03A9."),e("Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",1,"Conversion to a voltmeter","a high resistance R in series with the galvanometer \xB7 V = I_g(G + R) \xB7 R = {V;I_g} \u2212 G \xB7 the resistance of the voltmeter is G + R (very large) \xB7 the voltmeter is joined in parallel with the part of the circuit \xB7 its reading is exact only if its resistance is much more than the resistance across which it is connected","Find the total resistance V/I_g and subtract G.","G = 100 \u03A9, I_g = 1 mA and range 10 V: R = 10 \xF7 0.001 \u2212 100 = 9900 \u03A9."),e("Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",2,"Checking a converted meter","compare the converted ammeter with a standard ammeter in series (or a converted voltmeter with a standard voltmeter in parallel) at several readings \xB7 the error in the reading = reading of the converted meter \u2212 standard reading \xB7 the percentage error = {error;full-scale value} \xD7 100 % \xB7 a sensitive galvanometer needs a rheostat to vary the current smoothly","Choose several points along the scale, note both readings, and plot one against the other. The line should have a slope of 1.","Converted 0\u20131 A ammeter reads 0.48 A while the standard reads 0.50 A: the error is \u22120.02 A, which is 2 % of full scale."),e("Diodes, multimeter and graphs",1,"I\u2013V characteristic of a p-n junction diode","forward bias: the current is almost zero up to the knee voltage (about 0.7 V for silicon, 0.3 V for germanium) and then rises quickly \xB7 reverse bias: only a very small leakage current (\u03BCA) up to the breakdown \xB7 the characteristic is non-linear (not Ohm\u2019s law) \xB7 the dynamic resistance r = {\u0394V;\u0394I} at a point of the forward curve \xB7 a series resistance in the forward circuit protects the diode","Use a milliammeter in the forward circuit and a microammeter in the reverse circuit. Take many points near the knee. Draw the graph with the forward side on the right.","Between 0.70 V and 0.75 V the current rises by 10 mA: r = 0.05 \xF7 0.010 = 5 \u03A9."),e("Diodes, multimeter and graphs",1,"Zener diode characteristic","in the forward bias it behaves as an ordinary diode \xB7 in the reverse bias the current is tiny until the Zener (breakdown) voltage V_z, then it increases sharply at nearly constant voltage \xB7 this property is used for voltage regulation \xB7 always use a series resistance to limit the current \xB7 a Zener diode regulator works with V_z across the load; the series resistance takes the extra voltage","Plot the reverse voltage against the reverse current. The very steep part of the curve is the Zener region, and V_z is read at the start of that region.","For a 5.6 V Zener, the reverse current is below 10 \u03BCA up to 5 V and then rises to several mA in a very small voltage range around 5.6 V."),e("Diodes, multimeter and graphs",1,"Using a multimeter","as a voltmeter: in parallel, as an ammeter: in series (move the leads to the current socket) \xB7 set the range first to the largest value, then reduce \xB7 as an ohmmeter: only with the component out of the circuit and no source connected \xB7 the DC and AC settings are separate \xB7 diode test: forward-biased reading is low (about 0.5 V to 0.7 V shown), the reverse reading is OL (open)","Select the quantity, then the largest range, then connect. Never connect an ohmmeter across a live supply.","To test a resistor marked 100 \u03A9 \xB1 5 %, the reading should be between 95 \u03A9 and 105 \u03A9; a reading of OL means the resistor is open."),e("Diodes, multimeter and graphs",1,"Identifying a diode, LED, resistor and capacitor","diode: low resistance one way and very high the other way (the cathode is marked by a band) \xB7 LED: same as a diode, but needs a higher forward voltage (about 1.8 V to 3 V) and glows in the forward direction in the test \xB7 resistor: a steady reading in both directions equal to its value \xB7 capacitor: the ohmmeter reading starts low and rises slowly towards OL as it charges (reverse the leads, and the effect repeats); a dead capacitor reads 0 (short) or stays at OL immediately (open) \xB7 a continuity test buzzes for a short circuit","Look at what the reading does as the leads are reversed or as time passes. Steady and the same in both ways means a resistor; one-way means a diode or LED; a rising reading means a capacitor.","A component reads 500 \u03A9 in one direction and OL in the other: a diode (or LED if it glows). A component whose reading rises from 2 k\u03A9 to OL over a few seconds: a capacitor."),e("Diodes, multimeter and graphs",1,"Plotting graphs and finding the slope","choose a scale so that the points fill the sheet (equal divisions per cm, easy values like 1, 2 or 5 per cm) \xB7 mark the points with a small dot or cross and draw a smooth best-fit line so that points lie on both sides \xB7 slope = {\u0394y;\u0394x} using two points on the LINE far apart, not two data points \xB7 the intercept is read at x = 0 (state when the origin is not shown) \xB7 the units of the slope are the units of y divided by the units of x","Label the axes with quantity and unit. Draw the triangle for the slope with a large base. A line that should pass through the origin but does not shows a constant (systematic) error.","A T\xB2\u2013L line passes through (0.20 m, 0.80 s\xB2) and (1.00 m, 4.00 s\xB2): slope = 3.20 \xF7 0.80 = 4.0 s\xB2/m, so g = 4\u03C0\xB2/4.0 = 9.87 m/s\xB2.","xspend")],look:["Vernier: LC = 1 MSD/N; reading = MSR + VSR \xD7 LC; true = observed \u2212 zero error (negative error is added)","Screw gauge: LC = pitch/divisions; reading = MSR + CSR \xD7 LC; closed reading above/below the line gives \u2213e","Errors: sum and difference add \u0394A + \u0394B; product and quotient add relative errors; power A\u207F gives n \xD7 relative error","Sig figs: sum by decimal places, product by significant figures; many oscillations reduce the error in T","Pendulum: g = 4\u03C0\xB2L/T\xB2, L = l + r; small amplitude; T\xB2\u2013L line has slope 4\u03C0\xB2/g","Searle: Y = MgL/(\u03C0r\xB2l); capillary: T = rh\u03C1g/2; Stokes: \u03B7 = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/9v","Resonance tube: v = 2f(L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081); e = (L\u2082 \u2212 3L\u2081)/2; mixtures: m_s c_s(T\u2081 \u2212 T) = (m_w c_w + w)(T \u2212 T\u2080)","Mirror 1/v + 1/u = 1/f; lens 1/v \u2212 1/u = 1/f; displacement f = (D\xB2 \u2212 d\xB2)/4D; slab n = sin i/sin r; prism n = sin((A + \u03B4m)/2)/sin(A/2)","Metre bridge R/S = l/(100 \u2212 l); \u03C1 = \u03C0d\xB2R/4L; potentiometer E\u2081/E\u2082 = l\u2081/l\u2082, r = R(l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082)/l\u2082","Galvanometer: G = RS/(R \u2212 S), k = E/[(R + G)\u03B8]; shunt S = I_gG/(I \u2212 I_g); series R = V/I_g \u2212 G; diode knee 0.7 V (Si)"],notes:[{h:"Vernier callipers and least count",items:["The least count is the smallest value an instrument can read.","Vernier LC = 1 MSD \xF7 N; the usual case is 0.1 mm = 0.01 cm.","Reading = main scale reading just before the zero + coinciding line \xD7 LC.","Positive zero error is subtracted; negative zero error is added.","Outside jaws give the length, inside jaws the internal diameter, the strip the depth.","Do not press the jaws on the object; take readings at different places."],fig:"xsvern"},{h:"Screw gauge and metre scale",items:["Pitch is the distance moved in one turn; LC = pitch \xF7 circular divisions.","Reading = linear scale (including the half mm mark) + CSR \xD7 LC.","Closed gauge: zero error is read on the circular scale; near 0 it is positive, near the end it is negative.","Turn only in one direction (backlash) and use the ratchet.","Metre scale: read both ends and subtract; keep the eye straight above the mark (parallax).","Spherometer: R = l\xB2/6h + h/2 and h from a flat plate reading."],fig:"xsscrew"},{h:"Errors and significant figures",items:["Mean absolute error = mean of |a \u2212 a\u1D62|; relative error = \u0394a/a; percentage error = \u0394a/a \xD7 100.","In sums and differences the absolute errors add.","In products and quotients the relative errors add; a power multiplies the relative error.","A systematic error has one sign and can be corrected; a random error is reduced by averaging.","Significant figures: decimal places for sums, significant figures for products.","Timing many oscillations reduces the least count error in T."]},{h:"Simple pendulum and graphs",items:["T = 2\u03C0\u221A(L/g) with L measured to the centre of the bob (thread + radius).","Use a small heavy bob, a thin thread and an angle of 10\xB0 to 15\xB0 or less.","Time 20 oscillations from the mean position and use a fiducial mark.","The T\xB2\u2013L line goes through the origin; the slope is 4\u03C0\xB2/g.","Plotting T\xB2 against the thread length alone cuts the length axis at \u2212r.","The seconds pendulum is about 99.3 cm long."],fig:"xspend"},{h:"Young\u2019s modulus, surface tension and viscosity",items:["Searle: Y = MgL/(\u03C0r\xB2l); a reference wire cancels temperature changes.","Take loading and unloading readings and plot load against extension.","Capillary rise: T = rh\u03C1g/2 (\u03B8 = 0), with h corrected by r/3.","Stokes: \u03B7 = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/9v; v is the terminal speed between two marks.","The viscosity of a liquid falls when the temperature rises.","The radius of the wire or the ball enters squared, so measure it carefully."]},{h:"Speed of sound and specific heat",items:["Resonance tube: closed pipe resonances at L + e = \u03BB/4, 3\u03BB/4, \u2026","v = 2f(L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081) does not need the end correction.","e = (L\u2082 \u2212 3L\u2081)/2 and e \u2248 0.6 r.","v increases with temperature and humidity.","Method of mixtures: heat lost by the solid = heat gained by the water and the calorimeter.","Reduce the loss of heat with a lid, a jacket, quick transfer and stirring."],fig:"xsres"},{h:"Mirror, lens, slab and prism",items:["Use the Cartesian sign convention for mirror and lens formulas.","The 1/u\u20131/v graph is a straight line; the intercepts give 1/f.","At u = v = 2f (lens) or u = v = R (mirror) the image is as large as the object.","Displacement method: f = (D\xB2 \u2212 d\xB2)/4D with D > 4f.","Slab: n = sin i/sin r; the sin i\u2013sin r slope is n.","Prism: the i\u2013\u03B4 curve is U-shaped; at \u03B4m, i = e and n = sin((A + \u03B4m)/2)/sin(A/2)."],fig:"xsid"},{h:"Metre bridge and potentiometer",items:["Metre bridge: R/S = l/(100 \u2212 l) with the unknown in the left gap.","Keep the balance point between 30 cm and 70 cm; interchange R and S to remove the end errors.","\u03C1 = \u03C0d\xB2R/4L; the diameter has the biggest effect on the error.","Potentiometer: E\u2081/E\u2082 = l\u2081/l\u2082 for the same wire and current.","At balance no current is drawn from the test cell, so it gives the true emf.","Internal resistance: r = R(l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082)/l\u2082."],fig:"xsmb"},{h:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",items:["Half deflection: G = RS/(R \u2212 S); R is large, so G \u2248 S.","Figure of merit k = E/[(R + G)\u03B8] is the current per division.","Ammeter: a small shunt S = I_gG/(I \u2212 I_g) in parallel.","Voltmeter: a large series resistance R = V/I_g \u2212 G.","An ammeter is joined in series and has a very low resistance; a voltmeter in parallel has a very high resistance.","Compare a converted meter with a standard one at several points."],fig:"xspot"},{h:"Diodes, multimeter and graphs",items:["Forward bias: knee voltage 0.7 V (Si) or 0.3 V (Ge); reverse bias: tiny leakage current.","Zener: sharp rise of reverse current at V_z; use it as a voltage regulator with a series resistance.","Multimeter: voltmeter in parallel, ammeter in series, ohmmeter on an isolated component.","Resistor: same both ways; diode and LED: one way; capacitor: reading rises towards OL.","Graphs: use the full sheet, draw a best-fit line, find the slope from two far-apart points on the line.","A line that should pass through the origin but does not shows a systematic error."]}],asked:[{t:"Least count of a vernier or a screw gauge",what:"The main scale division, the number of vernier divisions or the pitch and the circular divisions are given. Find the least count or the length of a division.",steps:["Vernier: LC = 1 MSD \xF7 N.","Screw gauge: LC = pitch \xF7 circular divisions.","Pitch = distance \xF7 number of turns."],ex:"1 MSD = 0.5 mm, 50 VSD: LC = 0.01 mm."},{t:"Reading of a vernier",what:"A figure or the numbers show the main scale reading and the coinciding vernier line. Find the length.",steps:["Take the main scale reading before the vernier zero.","Find the coinciding line number.","Reading = MSR + VSR \xD7 LC."],ex:"23 mm and the 6th line: 23.6 mm.",fig:"xsvern"},{t:"Reading of a screw gauge with zero error",what:"The linear scale, the circular scale and the closed-gauge reading are given. Find the true diameter.",steps:["Reading = MSR + CSR \xD7 LC.","Find e from the closed gauge, with its sign.","True reading = observed \u2212 e."],ex:"4.78 mm with e = +0.03 mm: 4.75 mm.",fig:"xsscrew"},{t:"Zero error and zero correction",what:"The closed vernier or screw gauge shows a reading. Say whether the error is positive or negative and find the correction.",steps:["Zero to the right or the circular zero below the line: positive.","Negative error: e = \u2212(N \u2212 n) \xD7 LC.","Correction = \u2212e; true = observed \u2212 e."],ex:"4th line, N = 10, vernier zero on the left: e = \u22120.6 mm, true = observed + 0.6 mm."},{t:"Percentage error in a formula",what:"Quantities with their errors are combined in a formula with powers. Find the percentage error in the result.",steps:["Write the powers of each quantity.","Add power \xD7 relative error for each.","Convert to a percentage."],ex:"x\xB2y\xB3/z with 1 %, 2 %, 3 %: 11 %."},{t:"Error in a sum, a difference or a quotient",what:"Two measured values with absolute errors are added, subtracted, multiplied or divided. Find the error in the result.",steps:["Sum or difference: add the absolute errors.","Product or quotient: add the relative errors.","State the answer as value \xB1 error."],ex:"V = 5.0 \xB1 0.1 V, I = 2.0 \xB1 0.05 A: R = 2.5 \u03A9 with a 4.5 % error."},{t:"Mean error and significant figures",what:"A series of readings is given. Find the mean, the mean absolute error and write the result with the right significant figures.",steps:["Find the mean.","Mean of the |deviations| is \u0394a.","Round the result to the decimal place of the error."],ex:"2.63, 2.56, 2.42, 2.71, 2.80: 2.62 \xB1 0.11."},{t:"Simple pendulum: g and the effect of changes",what:"The length, the number of oscillations and the time are given. Find T and g, or say how a change of the bob, the amplitude or the length changes T.",steps:["T = t \xF7 n.","g = 4\u03C0\xB2L/T\xB2 with L = l + r.","T depends on L and g only."],ex:"1.00 m, 20 oscillations in 40.2 s: g = 9.77 m/s\xB2.",fig:"xspend"},{t:"T\xB2\u2013L graph of a pendulum",what:"A straight line is drawn or points are given. Find g from the slope, or say what an intercept means.",steps:["Slope = \u0394T\xB2/\u0394L.","g = 4\u03C0\xB2 \xF7 slope.","An intercept on the L axis is a constant error in L (such as r)."],ex:"Slope 4.0 s\xB2/m: g = 9.87 m/s\xB2.",fig:"xspend"},{t:"Young\u2019s modulus by Searle\u2019s method",what:"The length, the radius of the wire, the load and the extension are given. Find Y, or its percentage error, or the slope of the graph.",steps:["Y = MgL/(\u03C0r\xB2l).","Slope of M\u2013l gives M/l.","Percentage error includes 2\u0394r/r."],ex:"2.0 m, 0.4 mm, 1 kg, 0.20 mm: 1.95 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2."},{t:"Surface tension by capillary rise",what:"The radius of the tube and the height of the rise are given. Find T, or the effect of a smaller radius.",steps:["T = rh\u03C1g/2.","h can be corrected by adding r/3.","A narrower tube gives a larger h."],ex:"r = 0.5 mm, h = 3 cm: 0.0735 N/m."},{t:"Viscosity by Stokes\u2019 method",what:"The size and density of the ball, the density of the liquid and the terminal speed are given. Find \u03B7, or compare two balls.",steps:["\u03B7 = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/9v.","Use SI units.","Terminal speed v \u221D r\xB2."],ex:"r = 1 mm, \u03C1 = 7800, \u03C3 = 1260, v = 0.016 m/s: 0.89 Pa s."},{t:"Resonance tube: speed of sound and end correction",what:"The frequency and two resonance lengths are given. Find the speed of sound, the wavelength or the end correction.",steps:["\u03BB = 2(L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081).","v = f\u03BB.","e = (L\u2082 \u2212 3L\u2081)/2."],ex:"512 Hz, 16.0 cm and 50.0 cm: 348 m/s, e = 1.0 cm.",fig:"xsres"},{t:"Specific heat by the method of mixtures",what:"The masses, the temperatures and the water equivalent are given. Find the specific heat of a solid or of a liquid.",steps:["Heat lost = heat gained.","Include the calorimeter (water equivalent).","Solve for the unknown c."],ex:"100 g, 100 \xB0C, 150 g water at 25 \xB0C, final 30 \xB0C: 0.110 cal/g \xB0C."},{t:"Focal length of a mirror or a lens from u and v",what:"Pairs of object and image distances or a graph are given. Find f, or the type of image and the intercepts.",steps:["Use the Cartesian sign convention.","1/f = 1/v + 1/u (mirror) or 1/v \u2212 1/u (lens).","The 1/v\u20131/u graph has intercepts 1/f."],ex:"u = \u221230 cm, v = +60 cm, convex lens: f = 20 cm."},{t:"Displacement method for a convex lens",what:"The distance between object and screen and the shift of the lens are given. Find f.",steps:["D must be more than 4f.","f = (D\xB2 \u2212 d\xB2)/4D.","Magnifications at the two positions multiply to 1."],ex:"D = 100 cm, d = 20 cm: 24 cm."},{t:"Refractive index of a slab or a prism",what:"The angles of incidence and refraction, or A and \u03B4m, or real and apparent depths, are given. Find n.",steps:["Slab: n = sin i/sin r.","Prism: n = sin((A + \u03B4m)/2)/sin(A/2).","Microscope: n = real depth/apparent depth."],ex:"A = 60\xB0, \u03B4m = 37\xB0: n = 1.50.",fig:"xsid"},{t:"Metre bridge: resistance and resistivity",what:"The known resistance and the balance length are given. Find the unknown resistance, or the resistivity from the wire dimensions.",steps:["R/S = l/(100 \u2212 l).","\u03C1 = \u03C0d\xB2R/4L.","Interchange R and S to remove end errors."],ex:"S = 3.0 \u03A9, l = 40 cm: R = 2.0 \u03A9.",fig:"xsmb"},{t:"Potentiometer: emfs and internal resistance",what:"Balance lengths for two cells, or with and without a shunt resistance, are given. Find the ratio of emfs or the internal resistance.",steps:["E\u2081/E\u2082 = l\u2081/l\u2082.","r = R(l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082)/l\u2082.","The driver emf must be more than the test emf."],ex:"l\u2081 = 60, l\u2082 = 50, R = 10 \u03A9: r = 2.0 \u03A9.",fig:"xspot"},{t:"Galvanometer resistance, figure of merit and conversion",what:"R, S, E and the deflection are given for the half deflection method, or a range is given for an ammeter or voltmeter. Find G, k, the shunt or the series resistance.",steps:["G = RS/(R \u2212 S) and k = E/[(R + G)\u03B8].","Shunt: S = I_gG/(I \u2212 I_g).","Series: R = V/I_g \u2212 G."],ex:"G = 100 \u03A9, I_g = 1 mA: 0\u20131 A needs 0.100 \u03A9 shunt; 0\u201310 V needs 9900 \u03A9."},{t:"Diode, Zener, LED and multimeter",what:"A characteristic curve or the readings of a multimeter are given. Identify the component, find the knee or breakdown voltage or the dynamic resistance.",steps:["A diode conducts one way; Zener breaks down in reverse.","Dynamic resistance = \u0394V/\u0394I.","Resistor: same both ways; capacitor: reading rises."],ex:"\u0394V = 0.05 V, \u0394I = 10 mA: 5 \u03A9."}],guide:{intro:"Experimental Skills gives 2 to 3 questions in JEE Mains: a vernier or screw gauge reading with zero error, the least count, a percentage error in a formula, and one question on a standard experiment (metre bridge, potentiometer, galvanometer conversion, diode, pendulum graph). The formulas are short and the marks are easy to get once the rules are clear. About 5 hours spread over 4 days is enough.",plan:[["Day 1 \xB7 75 min","Least count, vernier and screw gauge readings, zero error and zero correction, metre scale."],["Day 2 \xB7 90 min","Errors: sum, product, power, least count error, significant figures; the pendulum and its T\xB2\u2013L graph."],["Day 3 \xB7 90 min","Searle, capillary rise, Stokes, resonance tube, calorimeter; mirror, lens, slab and prism."],["Day 4 \xB7 90 min","Metre bridge, potentiometer, galvanometer conversion, diode and Zener, multimeter, graphs; then the Most asked types."]],basics:["Units and conversion of mm, cm and m.","Ohm\u2019s law and series and parallel resistances.","Wheatstone bridge balance.","Mirror and lens formula with the sign convention.","Straight-line graphs: slope and intercept.","Basic ideas of heat: Q = mc\u0394T and the water equivalent."],path:[{t:"Step 1 \xB7 Instruments (Day 1)",d:"Learn the least count of the vernier and screw gauge and practise ten readings with zero errors of both signs.",ready:"you can read a vernier or a screw gauge and correct the zero error without a hint.",go:"formulas"},{t:"Step 2 \xB7 Errors (Day 2)",d:"Learn the rules for sum, product and power, and the rounding of significant figures.",ready:"you can find the percentage error of x\xB2y\xB3/z in one line.",go:"formulas"},{t:"Step 3 \xB7 Pendulum and graphs (Day 2)",d:"Learn T = 2\u03C0\u221A(L/g), the conditions of the experiment and the slope of the T\xB2\u2013L graph.",ready:"you can find g from a slope and say what a non-zero intercept means.",go:"notes"},{t:"Step 4 \xB7 Mechanics, sound and heat experiments (Day 3)",d:"Learn the formulas of Searle, capillary rise, Stokes, the resonance tube and the calorimeter.",ready:"you can find v and the end correction from L\u2081 and L\u2082.",go:"formulas"},{t:"Step 5 \xB7 Optics and electricity experiments (Day 3\u20134)",d:"Learn the u\u2013v and displacement methods, the prism curve, the metre bridge, the potentiometer and the galvanometer.",ready:"you can find R, r, G and the shunt in under a minute each.",go:"notes"},{t:"Step 6 \xB7 Practise by type",d:"Go through the Most asked types and solve the examples again without looking.",ready:"you solve every example in this chapter correctly twice in a row.",go:"asked"}],think:["Which instrument is it, and what is its least count?","Is there a zero error, and what is its sign? Correct every reading.","Is the quantity a sum, a product, or does it have a power? Choose the error rule.","Are the units SI (m, kg, s) before the numbers are put in the formula?","For a graph, write the equation of the line first and decide what the slope and the intercept mean.","Which condition of the experiment is broken (small angle, terminal speed, balance near the middle) and what error does it cause?","Is the answer reasonable (g near 9.8, n near 1.5, a resistance of a few ohm)?"],watch:["Do not mix up the sign of the zero error: a negative error is added to the observed reading.","The screw gauge reading includes the half-millimetre mark if it is visible.","The absolute errors add in a difference; they do not subtract.","The relative errors add in a quotient; they do not subtract.","The bob radius is added to the thread length in the pendulum.","Take the slope from two points on the line, not from two data points.","In the resonance tube e is not needed if \u03BB = 2(L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081) is used.","For a lens, 1/v \u2212 1/u = 1/f; for a mirror, 1/v + 1/u = 1/f."],myths:[["Myth: The least count of a vernier is the length of one vernier division.","Truth: It is the difference between one main scale division and one vernier division, 1 MSD \xF7 N."],["Myth: A positive zero error is added to the reading.","Truth: The true reading is the observed reading minus the zero error, so a positive error is subtracted."],["Myth: A larger number of readings removes a zero error.","Truth: Averaging reduces random errors only. A systematic error has to be corrected."],["Myth: In a quotient the relative errors cancel.","Truth: They add, because both quantities can go the wrong way together."],["Myth: The time period of a pendulum depends on the mass of the bob.","Truth: It depends on L and g only (for a small amplitude)."],["Myth: The T\xB2\u2013L graph must be drawn through the data points.","Truth: Draw the best-fit line with points on both sides and read the slope from two points on the line."],["Myth: The potentiometer reads the terminal voltage of the cell.","Truth: At balance no current flows through the cell, so it gives the emf."],["Myth: A voltmeter needs a low resistance in series.","Truth: A voltmeter needs a high resistance in series with the galvanometer; an ammeter needs a low resistance in parallel."],["Myth: The metre bridge works equally well at any balance length.","Truth: It is most accurate near the middle of the wire, at 40 cm to 60 cm."],["Myth: A diode obeys Ohm\u2019s law.","Truth: The I\u2013V curve is non-linear; there is a knee voltage and the resistance changes with the voltage."]]},extra:[]}})()})();
